INTRODUCTION TO FORMS IN REACT - OVERVIEW
================================================================================
LESSON OVERVIEW
================================================================================

Forms are used to collect user input such as:

- Text
- Email
- Password
- Selections
- Checkboxes
- Radio buttons

Common examples:

- Login
- Registration
- Search
- Contact forms
- Checkout forms

Key Difference:

Vanilla JavaScript
-> Form values are commonly accessed directly from the DOM.

React
-> Form values are commonly managed using component state.


================================================================================
1. WHY FORMS ARE DIFFERENT IN REACT
================================================================================

VANILLA JAVASCRIPT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Data Storage:
-> DOM

Access Value:
-> document.getElementById()

Update Value:
-> Direct DOM manipulation

Form Reset:
-> form.reset()

Validation:
-> Manual DOM checks


REACT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Data Storage:
-> Component state

Access Value:
-> State variable

Update Value:
-> State setter function

Form Reset:
-> Reset state to initial values

Validation:
-> State-based validation


Key Idea:

React State -> UI

The state becomes the main source of truth for controlled form elements.


================================================================================
2. CONTROLLED COMPONENTS
================================================================================

A controlled component is a form element whose value is controlled by
React state.

Basic Example:

import { useState } from 'react';

function SimpleForm() {

  const [name, setName] = useState('');

  const handleChange = (e) => {
    setName(e.target.value);
  };

  return (
    <div>

      <input
        type="text"
        value={name}
        onChange={handleChange}
        placeholder="Enter your name"
      />

      <p>You entered: {name}</p>

    </div>
  );
}

export default SimpleForm;


HOW IT WORKS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Create State

   const [name, setName] = useState('');

2. User types in the input.

3. onChange event fires.

4. Get the entered value:

   e.target.value

5. Update state:

   setName(e.target.value);

6. React re-renders the component.

7. Input receives the updated value:

   value={name}


Simple Flow:

User Types
    |
    v
onChange
    |
    v
handleChange()
    |
    v
e.target.value
    |
    v
setName()
    |
    v
State Updated
    |
    v
Component Re-renders
    |
    v
Input Shows New Value


