3. FORM SUBMISSION
================================================================================

Use the onSubmit event to handle form submission.

Important:

e.preventDefault();

prevents the browser's default form submission behavior, which would
normally reload/navigate the page.


Example:

import { useState } from 'react';

function SubmitForm() {

  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({
    name: '',
    email: '',
    message: ''
  });


  const handleChange = (e) => {

    const { name, value } = e.target;

    setFormData({
      ...formData,
      [name]: value
    });
  };


  const handleSubmit = (e) => {

    e.preventDefault();

    console.log('Form submitted:', formData);

  };


  return (

    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>

      <input
        type="text"
        name="name"
        value={formData.name}
        onChange={handleChange}
        placeholder="Name"
      />

      <input
        type="email"
        name="email"
        value={formData.email}
        onChange={handleChange}
        placeholder="Email"
      />

      <textarea
        name="message"
        value={formData.message}
        onChange={handleChange}
        placeholder="Message"
      />

      <button type="submit">
        Submit
      </button>

    </form>
  );
}

export default SubmitForm;


================================================================================
4. HANDLING MULTIPLE INPUTS
================================================================================

Instead of creating separate state variables and handlers for every field,
we can use one state object.

Example:

const [formData, setFormData] = useState({
  firstName: '',
  lastName: '',
  email: ''
});


Create one common handler:

const handleChange = (e) => {

  const { name, value } = e.target;

  setFormData({
    ...formData,
    [name]: value
  });
};


Inputs:

<input
  name="firstName"
  value={formData.firstName}
  onChange={handleChange}
/>

<input
  name="lastName"
  value={formData.lastName}
  onChange={handleChange}
/>

<input
  name="email"
  value={formData.email}
  onChange={handleChange}
/>


HOW IT WORKS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose the user changes:

name="email"


React gets:

name  = "email"
value = "user@example.com"


Then:

[name]: value

becomes:

email: "user@example.com"


The spread operator:

...formData

preserves the other existing fields.


Benefits:

- One handler for multiple inputs
- Less duplicate code
- Easy to maintain
- Scales well for larger forms


